{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## Cancelled a branch-head run by dispatching without looking first -- harmless by luck, not by judgement\n\nMonitor reported MAIN-cancelled on run 33874909241. That was my doing: test.yml's concurrency group\ncancels in-progress runs on the same ref, and my workflow_dispatch at 12:54 superseded a run that\nstarted at 12:51.\n\nChecked rather than assumed, and it cost nothing: the cancelled run was a SCHEDULED run at\n69cd2b9e15aa, which PREDATES 2618 (merged 12:53). It was testing a tree WITHOUT the fix, so it could\nnot have answered the question I dispatched for. My run 33875177644 is at 1af45065465e, which does\ncontain 2618 -- verified by merge-base ancestry, not by timestamp.\n\nBut that is luck. Had the scheduled run started three minutes later it would have been testing the\nright tree and I would have killed the answer I was waiting for, then waited another 45 minutes for my\nown. I noted this exact concurrency behaviour earlier tonight ('one branch-head dispatch at a time')\nand then dispatched without checking whether one was already in flight. Knowing a constraint and\nbuilding the habit that respects it are separate things -- the same gap that produced the blind\nmonitor and the patchset-base filter bug.\n\nHabit for the rest of this run: before dispatching test.yml, check 'gh run list --workflow=test.yml\n--branch main' for an in-progress run and its headSha. If one is already testing the tree I care\nabout, wait for it instead of restarting the clock."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788526616101042001,"uuid":"655dca94-f8ab-409a-b7cf-f40e7364646e"}